I say that the sum of any two angles of the triangle {ABC} is less than two right angles.

Produce {BC} to {D}. [!just I.Post.2]

[!just I.16; C.N.]

Since the angle {ACD} is an exterior angle of the triangle {ABC}, therefore it is greater than the interior and opposite angle {ABC|CBA}. Add the angle {ACB} to each. Then the sum of the angles {ACD} and {ACB} is greater than the sum of the angles {ABC|CBA} and {BCA}.

But the sum of the angles {ACD} and {ACB} is equal to two right angles. Therefore the sum of the angles {ABC|CBA} and {BCA} is less than two right angles. [!just I.13]

Similarly we can prove that the sum of the angles {BAC} and {ACB} is also less than two right angles, and so the sum of the angles {CAB} and {ABC|CBA} as well.

Therefore *in any triangle the sum of any two angles is less than two right angles.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

The statements

> ... the angle {ACD} ... is greater than the interior and opposite angle {ABC|CBA}. Add the angle {ACB} to each. Then the sum of the angles {ACD} and {ACB} is greater than the sum of the angles {ABC|CBA} and {BCA}.

use the property of magnitudes

<div class="math display">\text{If } x > y \text{, then } x + z > y + z\text{.}</div>

This property is not listed among the [Common Notions](../../commonnotions/cn1/).

This proposition is strengthened in Proposition @I.32 to say the sum of all three angles in a triangle equals two right angles.

#### Use of Proposition 17

This proposition is used in @III.16 and a couple other propositions of Books III, and a few in Books VI and XI.
